+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+The existing review flow spans the public product controller, authenticated customer controller, merchant product controller, review facades/services, DTO populators, and product read models. Review persistence currently uses a floating-point rating and existing exception mappings are not sufficient for all requested API outcomes.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Enforce privacy at the public DTO boundary.
+- Validate ownership, rating domain, and duplicate reviews before persistence.
+- Scope merchant lookup by both product and review id.
+- Centralize recalculation so create, update, and delete cannot leave stale product aggregates.
+- Add focused endpoint/service tests for success, rejection, and aggregate transitions.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- Changing authentication, authorization roles, review database schema, or unrelated customer reviews.
+- Backfilling historical aggregate values outside paths touched by review mutations.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- Use a dedicated public review representation/populator containing only the five permitted values. This is safer than serializing an entity and trying to hide fields with annotations, because customer relationships and addresses cannot leak through future serializer changes.
+- Validate the request rating as an integral numeric value in the controller/facade boundary, then normalize it to the existing review model. Reject before any service create/update call so failed requests cannot persist partial state.
+- Compare the request customer id with the authenticated principal/customer context before duplicate lookup and persistence. Return typed HTTP errors through the existing REST exception mechanism; use conflict for duplicate reviews and forbidden for identity mismatch.
+- Resolve merchant mutations with a product-and-review query or an explicit product ownership check before mutation. Missing and cross-product ids therefore share the required 404 behavior.
+- Recompute aggregates from the authoritative review collection after each successful mutation. Calculate with `BigDecimal` and `RoundingMode.HALF_UP`, set both zero values for an empty collection, and persist the product in the same service operation where practical. This favors correctness over incremental arithmetic that can drift after edits/deletes.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Risk] Existing clients may depend on extra public customer fields → [Mitigation] the requested privacy contract intentionally removes them; add response-shape tests to make the boundary explicit.
+- [Risk] Recalculation adds a review query per mutation → [Mitigation] mutations are low-frequency and correctness is more important; retain service-level aggregation so reads remain inexpensive.
+- [Risk] Legacy data may contain ratings outside 1–5 → [Mitigation] validate new and edited values while aggregate calculation remains defensive and tests cover the supported domain.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+Deploy code and tests without a schema migration. New review mutations refresh aggregates; existing stale products are corrected when their reviews are next changed. Rollback is a normal application rollback because persistence formats remain compatible.
